Z||000000||Jaei Sun||04-14-2001||02:32 AM||||I'm in the process of flushing out my system and bleeding it, I went with DOT 4 (the non synthetic, non silicone ver.)  And I'm wondering if anyone else has had luck with the DOT 4??<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||2
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||04-14-2001||09:52 AM||||I run dot4 because the dot 3 boils in my slave cylinder.  So far no ill effects.  I only had it in for 5 months. <P>Later<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-14-2001||03:43 PM||||DOT 3 & 4 are the same glycol base the main dif is additives. Myself, I would put Valvoline synthetic in things now. It has a dry boil temp around the same as DOT 5 silicone but is compatible with DOT 3&4. That means you get the benifit of high temp fluid without the silcone problems.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||04-14-2001||11:01 PM||||would there be a problem mixing the two?  and what about (stock) brakes, still ok to use DOT 4?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-14-2001||11:36 PM||||I haven't had allot of time to check out DOT 4. Again... the main reason you change fluid is:<P>1. the existing fluid is contaminated.<P>2. you need higher dry boil temp.<P>While DOT 4 is the same base as DOT 3, I'm not sure it's additive package is 100% compatitible with DOT 3. (Additives vary by brand, so that's a factor as well.) A reaction of the additives could be as bad as any other contaminant.<P>I would use one of the newer "high temp" DOT 3 formulas or Valvoline Synthetic. These are both 100% compatible with all older DOT 3 formulas. As long as the fluid specifically states it is DOT 3 compatible it should be safe to use.<P>The stuff I'm using now is STP "Super Heavy Duty" DOT 3. It has a dry boil rating of 500F. How much higher can you get? Valvoline's synthetic is only a little higher... I have it in my car and my 5000# caravan.<P>the main thing is DRY. you need to flush all the old crap you can from the system, but even wet the boil point is nearly 300F on most fluids.....||||reg||1
